1
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:10,710
So welcome, everybody, to the
final day of this workshop.

2
00:00:10,710 --> 00:00:16,400
So essentially up to now we
have been dealing with what's

3
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:17,840
known as the forward problem.

4
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,380
We have been building models
and then simulating them forward

5
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:25,230
in time and plotting
simulation results.

6
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:27,620
But for this last
session, we will

7
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:29,100
work on the inverse problem.

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:29,850
And that's it.

9
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:36,080
Given a model and
given some data,

10
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:39,990
how can we retrieve or
fit the model to the data

11
00:00:39,990 --> 00:00:43,340
so we can retrieve model
parameters that actually when

12
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:46,700
the model simulates, then
the result of the simulation

13
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:47,790
matches that data?

14
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:52,850
And dynamic causal modeling
is a way of doing this.

15
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:55,850
And it can essentially
accommodate

16
00:00:55,850 --> 00:01:00,480
any type of time series that we
typically use in neuroscience.

17
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:04,040
So most common cases will
be electrophysiological data

18
00:01:04,040 --> 00:01:05,850
or imaging data.

19
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:12,500
So fMRI needs experiments
heavily make use of DCM.

20
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,590
And as I said, the challenge
is to find the best model

21
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:19,580
parameters for your
model to make your model

22
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:21,030
look like the data.

23
00:01:21,030 --> 00:01:27,230
And DCM does that in
a Bayesian manner.

24
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:29,760
It follows at least
this Bayesian formalism.

25
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,700
And I'll talk more
about this in a second.

26
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:35,510
And it's using
state space models.

27
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:37,500
I'll show you an example
of state space model.

28
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,910
But you can think of them
as a discretized version

29
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:42,360
of the differential equations.

30
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:46,550
In state space models, we have
the state at one time point

31
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:49,650
and how that depends on states
at the previous time points.

32
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:53,480
So we have this almost
discrete evolution in steps.

33
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:56,960
And it's also a
method that gives us

34
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,660
back effective Connectivity
and especially if you're

35
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:04,290
coming from the
neuroimaging field,

36
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:06,310
you will probably be
more familiar with this.

37
00:02:06,310 --> 00:02:09,449
But what do we mean by
effective connectivity

38
00:02:09,449 --> 00:02:12,180
is essentially a
metric of connectivity

39
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:16,000
between regions of the brain
that is driven by a model.

40
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,330
So it is more than
just correlations

41
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:22,870
between those regions.

42
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:24,910
So for instance
here on the right,

43
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:28,320
we see some voxel-based
activity out

44
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,740
of three regions in the brain.

45
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:34,290
And typically, an
easy thing to do

46
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:37,080
is to calculate the functional
connectivity between them, which

47
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,960
is just taking correlations
between pairs of these,

48
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:44,970
and then seeing how well
correlated or anti-correlated

49
00:02:44,970 --> 00:02:46,240
these regions are.

50
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:47,950
But that's a purely
statistical measure.

51
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:52,720
With DCM, we add a
model into the mix.

52
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:55,470
And this model comes
from our hypothesis

53
00:02:55,470 --> 00:02:57,910
about the dynamics of
each one of these regions.

54
00:02:57,910 --> 00:03:02,230
So when we actually build
the final graph of the model,

55
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:04,900
as you can see here
with these four regions,

56
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:08,150
and we add any input, like
the U1's and the U2's here,

57
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:11,070
the inputs can be either
external inputs completely

58
00:03:11,070 --> 00:03:12,310
external to the brain.

59
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:16,350
Or they just can be inputs
from inside the brain

60
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:18,510
that we're not modeling,
like we did yesterday

61
00:03:18,510 --> 00:03:20,010
with the Poisson spike trains.

62
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:22,592
Those spike trains are
coming from inside the brain.

63
00:03:22,592 --> 00:03:24,300
It's just they're
coming from populations

64
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:27,120
that we don't model explicitly.

65
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:33,360
So when you build a model, like
this a more biophysical model,

66
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:36,270
and you feed this model to
the data, what you get back

67
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:39,390
is the effective
connectivity, and that's

68
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:42,390
a more reliable measure, I would
say, of the actual connectivity

69
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:45,210
between these regions
because it doesn't just

70
00:03:45,210 --> 00:03:46,930
rely on summary statistics.

71
00:03:46,930 --> 00:03:50,940
It looks into the dynamics of
the neuron over time and figures

72
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,720
out how they are connected.

73
00:03:54,720 --> 00:04:00,660
So as I was saying, DCM, and
particularly the spectral DCM

74
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:05,290
that we will look into now, is
built of state space models.

75
00:04:05,290 --> 00:04:08,160
And here you can see an
example of a state space

76
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,050
model expressed as a
differential equation

77
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,540
at this time.

78
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:15,210
And this is a bilinear
model because it

79
00:04:15,210 --> 00:04:18,870
has this the linear term,
which is the a times x.

80
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:23,890
But it also has this bilinear
term of the u times v times x.

81
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:28,740
So as I said, u's our inputs,
or inputs from populations

82
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:30,930
that we don't explicitly model.

83
00:04:30,930 --> 00:04:33,450
c is the input weights.

84
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:36,330
b, this bilinear
term is essentially

85
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:39,700
the effective connectivity as
it is modulated by the inputs.

86
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:41,920
That's why we have
the input multiplier.

87
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,610
And a is the
effective connectivity

88
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:47,590
between regions given no input.

89
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:49,830
This is independent of inputs.

90
00:04:49,830 --> 00:04:54,960
And the main goal of
DCM is to give you

91
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:57,220
back these a and b matrices.

92
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:00,990
So this is how you can tell
how your network is actually

93
00:05:00,990 --> 00:05:03,480
structured by looking
into the a if you care

94
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:07,290
about the case of connectivity
without any inputs

95
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:10,660
or looking into the
combination of a and b,

96
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:13,350
if you also care about
some specific inputs.

97
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:15,270
Now, the assumptions
that it makes

98
00:05:15,270 --> 00:05:18,000
is that it assumes that
there is a weak stationarity

99
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:18,850
in your signal.

100
00:05:18,850 --> 00:05:22,950
And that means that the cross
spectral density, essentially,

101
00:05:22,950 --> 00:05:27,760
for any finite amount of
measured time that you have,

102
00:05:27,760 --> 00:05:32,580
say, in a bold experiment
that remains constant,

103
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:34,870
it assumes bilinearity.

104
00:05:34,870 --> 00:05:37,530
And this is essentially the
introduction of this b matrix

105
00:05:37,530 --> 00:05:41,470
here, where it allows for this
input modulated connectivity.

106
00:05:41,470 --> 00:05:46,260
And also it assumes
Gaussianity, and that basically

107
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:49,650
means that all of the
parameters that we're

108
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:52,800
fitting during the
DCM process, we

109
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,960
assume that they come from
a normal distribution.

110
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:58,880
And that's, in other words,
the Laplace approximation.

111
00:06:01,810 --> 00:06:04,630
Now, here I'd like to show
you essentially on the left

112
00:06:04,630 --> 00:06:13,060
how a typical pipeline of
DCM works on both data.

113
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:17,770
And on the right, we have the
equivalent snippets of code

114
00:06:17,770 --> 00:06:19,070
from Neuroblox itself.

115
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:22,750
So we start from the
actual neural dynamics.

116
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:25,480
And our default is to
use this neural mass

117
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:28,990
model, which is exactly this
equation here or written

118
00:06:28,990 --> 00:06:29,630
in Neuroblox.

119
00:06:29,630 --> 00:06:34,000
Here you see this
dx equals to jcn

120
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,580
because, as you see in previous
days, jcn can be any input.

121
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:40,360
So as we build the
circuit stitch together

122
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:42,460
multiple of these
linear mass models,

123
00:06:42,460 --> 00:06:46,420
essentially we will build
the a matrix and the b matrix

124
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:50,180
and any c matrix if
that's also included.

125
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:53,530
And now below that,
so the result of this

126
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:57,530
is some population
average neural activity.

127
00:06:57,530 --> 00:06:59,030
This is just a
neural mass model.

128
00:06:59,030 --> 00:07:03,280
But then we move into the
hemodynamic state equations.

129
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:06,740
So this is what is
called the balloon model.

130
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:11,020
So this model will
essentially model the dilation

131
00:07:11,020 --> 00:07:14,350
of blood vessels, and
it will model blood flow

132
00:07:14,350 --> 00:07:18,800
in the brain, given
parameters of the neural mass,

133
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:21,310
specifically for this
region that we're

134
00:07:21,310 --> 00:07:22,670
modeling with the neural mass.

135
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:27,557
And on top of the balloon model,
we have this the BOLD signal.

136
00:07:27,557 --> 00:07:29,890
So essentially, we take the
results of the balloon model

137
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:32,590
and we transform them to
BOLD, which is actually

138
00:07:32,590 --> 00:07:34,670
a quantity measured in fMRI.

139
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:36,670
So this is essentially
the pipeline

140
00:07:36,670 --> 00:07:43,850
that helps us move from a neural
mass scale all the way up,

141
00:07:43,850 --> 00:07:46,790
essentially, the
hierarchy to BOLD.

142
00:07:46,790 --> 00:07:50,530
And we can relate our model,
our biophysical model,

143
00:07:50,530 --> 00:07:58,090
it can be any blocks here
to actual imaging data.

144
00:07:58,090 --> 00:08:01,240
And here I'm also showing
you the symbolic way

145
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:02,980
that we write the
balloon equations

146
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:05,987
to see that it actually
matches the math quite easily.

147
00:08:05,987 --> 00:08:07,570
So of course, the
balloon model is not

148
00:08:07,570 --> 00:08:11,830
the only model that can actually
transform neural activity

149
00:08:11,830 --> 00:08:12,980
to BOLD.

150
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:15,680
Eventually you can replace
this with other models.

151
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:21,820
So this is what we call
an observer blocks.

152
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:24,010
Now, in order to solve
the inverse problem

153
00:08:24,010 --> 00:08:31,790
and get back the true parameters
for your network in DCM,

154
00:08:31,790 --> 00:08:35,690
we perform variational
Bayesian approach.

155
00:08:35,690 --> 00:08:37,720
So in other words,
that means that we

156
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:41,200
assume a specific
family of specific shape

157
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:44,000
of the distribution
for the posterior.

158
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,090
And the posterior
distribution in Bayesian terms

159
00:08:46,090 --> 00:08:49,010
is always the distribution of
the parameters given the data.

160
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:51,400
And this is Bayes' rule.

161
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,750
That's equal to a
likelihood function, that

162
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:57,425
is, the probability of the data
given parameters and any prior

163
00:08:57,425 --> 00:08:58,800
that we have over
the parameters.

164
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:00,390
So that's the p of probability.

165
00:09:00,390 --> 00:09:03,720
And yeah, this product
is the important thing.

166
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:06,440
And this product is divided by
this normalizing factor, which

167
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:07,980
is the probability of data i.

168
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:11,270
But in practical applications,
because the probability of data

169
00:09:11,270 --> 00:09:15,140
is something very hard to
calculate because that assumes

170
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:18,290
no specific parameters, that's
the probability of all data

171
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:22,910
in your experiment, we apply
this variational Bayes' trick,

172
00:09:22,910 --> 00:09:27,770
which helps us circumvent
essentially the computation

173
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:31,940
of this because we assume a
specific shape of the posterior.

174
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:35,270
Now, after making
this assumption,

175
00:09:35,270 --> 00:09:38,990
we will perform essentially
an optimization.

176
00:09:38,990 --> 00:09:41,630
And this optimization
is to maximize

177
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:44,330
the negative free energy,
which, in other words,

178
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:46,580
in machine learning literature,
you can also find this

179
00:09:46,580 --> 00:09:48,470
as the evidence lower bound.

180
00:09:48,470 --> 00:09:50,900
It's this quantity
right here, which,

181
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:54,080
if you expand on the log a
bit, you can see that it also--

182
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:57,540
it's the same as the
Kullback-Leibler divergence,

183
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:00,200
which is a function that
measures the distance

184
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:01,470
between two distributions.

185
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:03,860
And in this case,
it's the distance

186
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:07,580
between q, which is
our assumption of what

187
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:13,090
the posterior looks like, and
essentially, the marginal--

188
00:10:13,090 --> 00:10:13,590
sorry.

189
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:14,507
It's not the marginal.

190
00:10:14,507 --> 00:10:17,690
This is the combination
essentially of the likelihood

191
00:10:17,690 --> 00:10:18,810
and the priors together.

192
00:10:18,810 --> 00:10:20,900
So essentially
we're trying to make

193
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:23,090
this what we have
assumed for this quantity

194
00:10:23,090 --> 00:10:26,365
as close to possible as the
product of these two here.

195
00:10:29,210 --> 00:10:31,400
And in order to
do this, we apply

196
00:10:31,400 --> 00:10:33,570
this Levenberg-Marquardt
optimization.

197
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:38,300
So this is what was recommended
by the original DCM work.

198
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:40,580
And it does perform better
than other approaches

199
00:10:40,580 --> 00:10:42,950
like a Gauss-Newton
method, which

200
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:45,420
is very common
optimization method.

201
00:10:45,420 --> 00:10:46,830
But as you can
see, for instance,

202
00:10:46,830 --> 00:10:48,900
in the case of this
posterior distribution,

203
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:50,630
if you apply
Gauss-Newton and you

204
00:10:50,630 --> 00:10:55,890
start from a point of no
high probability density,

205
00:10:55,890 --> 00:10:59,220
then this method can drift away,
and it can lead you astray,

206
00:10:59,220 --> 00:10:59,730
basically.

207
00:10:59,730 --> 00:11:01,480
But if you follow the
Levenberg-Marquardt,

208
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:03,600
even from the same
initial point,

209
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:08,600
it actually manages to find the
place of the highest probability

210
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:14,090
mass, which is what you would
want ideally from this method.

211
00:11:14,090 --> 00:11:19,740
Now, there are different
types of the DCM analysis.

212
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:25,100
The earliest one we had the
deterministic DCM, which

213
00:11:25,100 --> 00:11:27,770
included simple state
space models like the one

214
00:11:27,770 --> 00:11:30,810
that I showed you,
then stochastic DCM,

215
00:11:30,810 --> 00:11:33,440
which can include
nonlinearities and also

216
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:36,030
include, well,
stochastic terms in it.

217
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:38,600
But exactly because
of the introduction

218
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:41,210
of the stochasticity, that
became computationally much

219
00:11:41,210 --> 00:11:43,040
heavier.

220
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:46,430
So in other words, it's
impossible to run a model

221
00:11:46,430 --> 00:11:48,650
with many different
regions in this model

222
00:11:48,650 --> 00:11:50,880
in any reasonable
amount of time.

223
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:54,060
A regression-based
DCM, on the other hand,

224
00:11:54,060 --> 00:11:57,010
is very fast because,
in regression DCM,

225
00:11:57,010 --> 00:12:02,260
we replace the state space
model with some simple GLM.

226
00:12:02,260 --> 00:12:04,440
That's the generalized
linear model.

227
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:07,870
So we do away with a lot of
the differential equations

228
00:12:07,870 --> 00:12:10,450
so that that's much
faster to compute.

229
00:12:10,450 --> 00:12:13,900
And the spectral DCM is
somewhere in between the others.

230
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:18,180
It allows for stochasticity.

231
00:12:18,180 --> 00:12:20,080
In terms of the
speed of running,

232
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:22,260
it's in between
the deterministic

233
00:12:22,260 --> 00:12:23,170
and the regression.

234
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:27,060
But it maintains
biophysical relevance

235
00:12:27,060 --> 00:12:29,950
because we still keep
the state space models.

236
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:32,070
And as I was saying,
you can add any blocks

237
00:12:32,070 --> 00:12:38,610
that you want to for any one
of your regions in your model.

238
00:12:38,610 --> 00:12:43,180
Now in Neuroblox, we have our
own implementation of DCM.

239
00:12:43,180 --> 00:12:47,820
DCM, it's also originally
comes from SPM,

240
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:52,840
which is a statistical package,
I believe originally in Matlab,

241
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:54,670
but there's also a
dual implementation.

242
00:12:54,670 --> 00:12:58,140
And here I'm just showing
you some performance graphs,

243
00:12:58,140 --> 00:13:02,430
where you can see that our
implementation in Julia,

244
00:13:02,430 --> 00:13:03,930
using modeling toolkit--

245
00:13:03,930 --> 00:13:07,450
that's in green-- or
using not modeling toolkit

246
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:10,350
but the nonsymbolic way of
writing differential equations

247
00:13:10,350 --> 00:13:12,870
but still using automatic
differentiation, which

248
00:13:12,870 --> 00:13:16,630
is actually the novelty here.

249
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:19,440
The original implementation
of DCM in SPM

250
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:22,470
does not have any
automatic differentiation.

251
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:25,530
So both of these
implementations, the towers,

252
00:13:25,530 --> 00:13:28,470
the orange and green are much
faster than the original one

253
00:13:28,470 --> 00:13:29,470
in Matlab.

254
00:13:29,470 --> 00:13:33,210
But they're also faster
than the original algorithm

255
00:13:33,210 --> 00:13:35,530
implemented in Julia.

256
00:13:35,530 --> 00:13:39,610
And the importance is that
the orange and green overlap.

257
00:13:39,610 --> 00:13:41,640
So that tells us
that, even though we

258
00:13:41,640 --> 00:13:44,020
use modeling toolkit,
which is the symbolic way,

259
00:13:44,020 --> 00:13:47,820
we don't lose performance over
the nonsymbolic way of writing

260
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:51,010
differential equations.

261
00:13:51,010 --> 00:13:55,233
And essentially, this is in
spirit with modeling toolkit

262
00:13:55,233 --> 00:13:56,650
and in spirit with
everything else

263
00:13:56,650 --> 00:13:57,980
that we've seen in Neuroblox.

264
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:02,780
We wrote SP-DCM to
be very modular.

265
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:06,700
So you can essentially follow
this entire pipeline, which you

266
00:14:06,700 --> 00:14:09,590
will in the exercises as well.

267
00:14:09,590 --> 00:14:14,030
And you can replace parts
of it with different parts.

268
00:14:14,030 --> 00:14:16,670
For instance, when you define
your neural mask block,

269
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:20,290
you can just replace, say,
this set of two equations

270
00:14:20,290 --> 00:14:23,410
with your own block.

271
00:14:23,410 --> 00:14:25,250
When you build the
circuit, of course,

272
00:14:25,250 --> 00:14:27,040
you have the freedom
to build any circuit

273
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:30,115
that you want that comes from
your hypothesis about which

274
00:14:30,115 --> 00:14:31,990
regions of the brain
you think are implicated

275
00:14:31,990 --> 00:14:33,670
in your experiment.

276
00:14:33,670 --> 00:14:37,180
You can provide any
measurements that you

277
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:42,280
like, be it electrophysiological
ones or imaging ones.

278
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:47,740
And then we give you the option
of doing variational inference

279
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:51,900
with automatic differentiation
and with the Laplace

280
00:14:51,900 --> 00:14:55,710
approximation and also
give you out some metrics

281
00:14:55,710 --> 00:14:58,680
so you can tell whether
the optimization worked

282
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:03,360
or how much you should trust
your results essentially.

283
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:05,470
And this is also
published from our group,

284
00:15:05,470 --> 00:15:08,850
so you can check this
preprint as well.

285
00:15:08,850 --> 00:15:11,370
So finally, I would like to
talk about model selection

286
00:15:11,370 --> 00:15:16,740
because say that we make an
assumption about the network

287
00:15:16,740 --> 00:15:21,810
that our experiments should
stimulate or so the network

288
00:15:21,810 --> 00:15:22,960
that should be involved.

289
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:24,780
But we don't exactly
have the connectivity

290
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:27,820
within that network, so we can
try different configurations.

291
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:31,140
So here you can see
the results of trying

292
00:15:31,140 --> 00:15:33,670
three different configurations
and comparing them.

293
00:15:33,670 --> 00:15:35,730
And to do the comparison
we essentially print out

294
00:15:35,730 --> 00:15:37,570
the free energy functional.

295
00:15:37,570 --> 00:15:42,790
So larger values for the
negative free energy are better.

296
00:15:42,790 --> 00:15:48,040
So the blue line is the ground
truth here, the ground truth

297
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:50,780
model, because this comes
from simulated data.

298
00:15:50,780 --> 00:15:53,440
So we used the blue one
to generate some data

299
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:54,770
and then fit all three.

300
00:15:54,770 --> 00:15:58,390
And then the orange is a
version of the ground truth

301
00:15:58,390 --> 00:16:00,740
model with just one
connection removed.

302
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:02,690
This was a model
with three regions,

303
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:06,220
but the orange one has the
connection from region 2

304
00:16:06,220 --> 00:16:07,520
to region 1 removed.

305
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:11,560
And model 3 has this connection
and also another connection

306
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:13,130
from 2 to 3 removed.

307
00:16:13,130 --> 00:16:15,430
So as you can see,
the comparison

308
00:16:15,430 --> 00:16:18,670
works because as you move
further and further away

309
00:16:18,670 --> 00:16:21,610
from the ground truth model,
the free energy functional

310
00:16:21,610 --> 00:16:23,740
becomes worse and worse.

311
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:25,990
And this is the
metric that we apply

312
00:16:25,990 --> 00:16:30,460
to do model selection at
the end of the DCM process.

313
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:32,240
So this was my final slide.

314
00:16:32,240 --> 00:16:35,950
So you can navigate
to the final session,

315
00:16:35,950 --> 00:16:38,050
spectral dynamic
causal modeling,

316
00:16:38,050 --> 00:16:42,280
and open the relevant
notebook DCM.

317
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:45,070
And yeah, feel free
to ask any questions.

318
00:16:45,070 --> 00:16:46,620
Thanks.

